// A tuple, which is the one product with no name and no `repr` to choose
// (Ch. 2 §3).
//
// Its layout is a `repr(lang)` struct's with the same fields and nothing
// else: permuted by alignment, and **unspecified** for exactly that reason.
// Code that needs a fixed order writes a struct, which is why there is no
// `repr(linear)` tuple to write.
//
// Which means the interesting cases are the ones where the permutation is
// visible — a narrow element before a wide one, so that what is written
// first is not what is at offset zero — and the niches, since a tuple lends
// an enclosing enum the same spare values a struct does.

struct P { a: t27, b: t9 }

// A word and then a tryte: nothing moves, and the size is the padding the
// alignment asks for.
struct Wide { x: (t27, t9) }

// The same two the other way round. The permutation puts the word first, so
// what was **written** first is at offset three — which is the whole of what
// "unspecified" buys and the whole of what it costs.
struct Narrow { x: (t9, t27) }

// Three of the same, where there is nothing to permute.
struct Even { x: (t9, t9, t9) }

// One element, which is a tuple and not the element: `(t27,)` is a product
// of one and `(t27)` would be a parenthesized type.
struct Single { x: (t27,) }

// An element that is itself laid out, and one that is itself a tuple.
struct Held { x: (P, t9) }

struct Inner { x: ((t9, t9), t27) }

// And the niches, which is where a layout is visible without being printed.

// `bool` leaves a tryte's patterns spare, so `None` takes one of them — at
// the **permuted** offset, which is three and not zero.
enum MaybePair { Some((bool, t27)), None }

// `t9` uses every pattern of its tryte, so a tuple of them lends nothing and
// the tag is stored.
enum MaybeBytes { Some((t9, t9)), None }

// A reference is never null, which a tuple passes on like any other field.
struct Ref { p: &t27 }

enum MaybeRef { Some((Ref, t9)), None }

fn main() -> t27 { 0 }
